WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:12.120
Welcome back to Python core and applied concepts for a level data sense workflow.

00:12.120 --> 00:19.020
In this session, we will discuss briefly about different types of programming error that

00:19.020 --> 00:24.360
occur more frequently when we try to write no programming in Python programming language

00:24.360 --> 00:28.160
or in general in any programming language.

00:28.160 --> 00:35.600
So before we go to the programming errors, let's pause for a moment on this slide and

00:35.600 --> 00:44.840
try to see a very important evolving pattern in today's programming paradigm.

00:44.840 --> 00:51.600
So this picture is trying to highlight the evolving role of a coder in contemporary

00:51.600 --> 00:58.600
programming practices where most of us are using LLM to generate our first draft of code.

00:58.600 --> 01:05.600
So if you think a little bit carefully, in past two decades, learning a programming language

01:05.600 --> 01:11.600
means learning the syntax and semantics of programming language and mostly we were

01:11.600 --> 01:15.600
used to writing our code from very splash.

01:16.600 --> 01:21.600
We used to learn the syntax and semantics and then try to write code for our purpose

01:21.600 --> 01:24.600
without any external help.

01:24.600 --> 01:30.600
But with the advent of LLM since these are models, probabilistic model trained on

01:30.600 --> 01:39.600
a large repository of code base, we somehow tend to believe on the code that has been

01:40.600 --> 01:45.600
generated by these LLMs and we normally query these LLM with natural language from to

01:45.600 --> 01:47.600
get our first draft of the code.

01:47.600 --> 01:54.600
So that's perfectly acceptable thing to get your first draft of code using these LLM.

01:54.600 --> 02:01.600
But then if you are a serious programmer, the owners of validation or making sure that

02:01.600 --> 02:07.600
the code behaves the way you wanted it to behave lies with you.

02:08.600 --> 02:13.600
Now with the presence of LLM where you are not investing your time in writing this

02:13.600 --> 02:19.600
code from scratch, it's even more desirable that you at least validate the code that has

02:19.600 --> 02:25.600
been generated by some like LLM or any other agent.

02:25.600 --> 02:33.600
So going ahead, our responsibility as a validator is even more since we are relying on

02:33.600 --> 02:39.600
a third party like LLM to generate our first piece of code.

02:39.600 --> 02:44.600
Now let's come back to the type of programming error that can occur when we are writing a

02:44.600 --> 02:45.600
program.

02:45.600 --> 02:48.600
The very first type of programming error are called syntax error.

02:48.600 --> 02:56.600
So this syntax error caused when you violate the syntax rule of the programming language.

02:56.600 --> 03:02.600
So let's say if you are trying to write a program which is not following one of the syntactical

03:02.600 --> 03:07.600
rule of creation of either loads of expression or a statement in the programming language, then

03:07.600 --> 03:10.600
you will certainly hit this syntax error.

03:10.600 --> 03:16.600
But the good news is that compilers are good at catching this syntax error mostly because

03:16.600 --> 03:21.600
this syntax error has been defined very formally in Python reference manual.

03:21.600 --> 03:27.600
That's how compiler writers can be very clear about what is accepted as a valid program

03:27.600 --> 03:29.600
and what is not accepted as a valid program.

03:29.600 --> 03:36.600
And hence compiler will complain and it will not let you go ahead with running your

03:36.600 --> 03:38.600
programs if there is a syntax error.

03:38.600 --> 03:44.600
So this is kind of a very easy type of error which are somewhere here which is easily

03:44.600 --> 03:45.600
visible to you.

03:45.600 --> 03:50.600
Even if you let's say make this mistake or a LLM make this mistake, compiler will complain

03:50.600 --> 03:53.600
and you will be able to identify this bug.

03:53.600 --> 03:58.600
And then repair from those bugs, you can actually debug these kind of bugs.

03:58.600 --> 04:02.600
But the other two types of bugs which are runtime errors and semantics error are

04:02.600 --> 04:09.600
a little difficult and syntax error to catch and even recover from.

04:09.600 --> 04:11.600
So what are runtime errors?

04:11.600 --> 04:19.600
So imagine a scenario where your compiler did not complain and it gave you a

04:19.600 --> 04:22.600
clinch to go ahead and then run your program.

04:22.600 --> 04:28.600
But since your compiler did not complain about any structural invalidity,

04:28.600 --> 04:33.600
it does not mean that your program will always run properly and complete its execution.

04:33.600 --> 04:41.600
Sometimes due to some corner cases of user input, it may crash at the runtime.

04:41.600 --> 04:47.600
For example, a very simple program to calculate the division of one number by another number.

04:47.600 --> 04:53.600
You might not check whether the denominator is non-zero.

04:53.600 --> 04:58.600
In that case, if you have written a function not taking into account this kind of scenario,

04:58.600 --> 05:02.600
then when a user gives argument where the second argument is zero,

05:02.600 --> 05:08.600
at the runtime your program will crash because division by zero is not defined.

05:08.600 --> 05:16.600
So this kind of error can also come and they cannot be caught at the compilation time

05:16.600 --> 05:22.600
because the program followed all the syntax rule of the language.

05:22.600 --> 05:28.600
But at the runtime it may produce some error or some crash which is unexpected.

05:28.600 --> 05:36.600
So that's why I mean to become a good programmer we should be able to not only predict

05:36.600 --> 05:42.600
this kind of runtime unexpected behavior but also we should take care of these

05:42.600 --> 05:48.600
unexpected behavior inside our code by writing proper exception handling mechanism

05:48.600 --> 05:52.600
or blocks which gracefully handle this kind of unexpected scenario.

05:52.600 --> 05:58.600
So these runtime errors are a little more subtle than syntax error,

05:58.600 --> 06:04.600
but still I mean with practice you can learn how to handle this kind of runtime error.

06:04.600 --> 06:08.600
Now coming to the third kind of error which we call semantic error.

06:08.600 --> 06:11.600
I mean this is the most difficult type of error.

06:11.600 --> 06:15.600
And the difficulty comes from again what we discussed in the previous class

06:15.600 --> 06:23.600
from the ambiguity in the semantics because you know the semantic rules are not that clear.

06:23.600 --> 06:28.600
So what we rely on is our overall understanding of what is the semantics of the program.

06:28.600 --> 06:32.600
So when you start writing a program you have some intention in your mind

06:32.600 --> 06:39.600
and you want to map that intention into a set of or sequencer instruction that you write in your program.

06:39.600 --> 06:45.600
Now let's say your compiler does not complain about the structural validity of the program

06:45.600 --> 06:49.600
and your program also runs to completion and produces result.

06:49.600 --> 06:55.600
So simply because it could run to completion and produce some result does not mean

06:55.600 --> 06:58.600
that the result is what you wanted to calculate.

06:58.600 --> 07:03.600
There can be a misalignment between what is produced as an output

07:03.600 --> 07:07.600
and what you intended to produce as an output.

07:07.600 --> 07:13.600
So this is called semantic error because I mean there is a gap between what you wanted program to calculate

07:13.600 --> 07:15.600
and what the program actually calculates.

07:15.600 --> 07:21.600
Although the program is syntax tickly correct and has no runtime error occurring,

07:21.600 --> 07:27.600
it does not guarantee that it will be solving the same problem that you wanted this program to solve.

07:27.600 --> 07:31.600
It will be computing the same value that you wanted it to compute.

07:31.600 --> 07:38.600
So this is the most difficult kind of error which may lie deep down your code

07:38.600 --> 07:47.600
and even most proficient coder often make this mistake and they cannot figure out these kind of bugs and then debug it.

07:47.600 --> 07:54.600
Now in conclusion what we want to emphasize is that it's very important to understand the precise semantics

07:54.600 --> 08:04.600
and this is probably the most desired skill going ahead when we are relying on LLM to produce our first draft of code.

08:04.600 --> 08:09.600
For watching this video I will see you in the next video till then goodbye.

